    "evidence_text": "Infection is regulated by hepatic immune defences triggered by the cellular RIG-I helicase.\n\nLoss of DExD/H box RNA helicase LGP2 manifests disparate antiviral responses.\n\nHowever, the function of the DExH box helicase domain that is also required for activity is less clear.\n\nThis functional integration of two RNA molecular patterns may provide a means to specifically sense and counteract replicating viruses.\n\nThis dialog may be affected by the individual's genetic background.\n\nPIC increased MDA5 and PTPN2 mRNA expression, which was inhibited by the specific siRNAs.\n\nThese findings indicate that changes in MDA5 and PTPN2 expression modify beta-cell responses to dsRNA.\n\nImmune signaling by RIG-I-like receptors.\n\nThe helicase DDX41 senses intracellular DNA mediated by the adaptor STING in dendritic cells\n\nThe recognition of pathogenic DNA is important to the initiation of antiviral responses.\n\nDDX41 bound both DNA and STING and localized together with STING in the cytosol.\n\nRIG-I-mediated antiviral responses to single-stranded RNA bearing 5'-phosphates.",
